Container vs VM
VM:
On top of hardware we install hypervisor and top of that we will create vm.in vm will have os+app and its lib and dependencies. We can create many vm on top of hypervisor. So it consumer more resources, because on hypervisor will be more different os so it consume high resources and load time is high and size is big too.
Container:
On container on single host os we can create any number of container and took less resource because it has single host os and container use the same kernal os so less resource and less size and boot time is quick.

So we work together : On server, we will create hypervisor, then on top we will create a vm , on vm will create multiple container. This how we will work both together.Earlier on vm one app and now one vm 1000 containers.

Images and registries:
Images:We will create all our application as image ie complete package or template. We can create our own image ie our application we can create as image. With image we can create multiple running containers  or instance.We can have postgres image and use that image and create a instance, so that instance will have pre installed postgres and postgres releated things.
Registery:Where we store all our images. Docker hub, github container registeris are examples.

Docker and Devops:
Earlier when developer develope the application they will give war file and installation guide to devops people where they manage application. But devops team will not have an developer knowledege so they need to reachout to developer to fix this issue. So with image developer can deveope the image and share with devops team so they can install in any environment.  

